---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How big is a byte, really? — five small explainers"
  description="A click-through ladder from a single character to all of Wikipedia, each step a giant leap in scale."
  accent="violet"
  activePath={`${base}byte-scale/`}
>
  <h1>How big is a byte, really?</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A single character is one byte. Click through eight steps and watch how
    fast that number runs away — each step is real data, from a single emoji
    to the entire text of Wikipedia.
  </p>

  <section class="panel ladder" aria-label="Scale ladder">
    <div class="trail" id="trail" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="scale-stage">
      <span class="current-icon" id="current-icon" aria-hidden="true">🔤</span>
    </div>

    <h2 id="rung-label">One text character</h2>
    <p class="rung-bytes" id="rung-bytes">1 byte</p>
    <p class="rung-fact" id="rung-fact" aria-live="polite"></p>

    <div class="btn-row ladder-controls">
      <button class="btn" id="back-btn" type="button" disabled>Back</button>
      <span id="step-status" class="step-status" aria-live="polite">Step 1 of 8</span>
      <button class="btn btn-primary" id="next-btn" type="button">Next</button>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="explainer">
    <h2>Why the jumps aren't even</h2>
    <p>
      Data size doesn't grow in neat, equal steps — it grows by whatever the
      content actually needs, and that makes some jumps huge and others
      barely noticeable. A text message is a few hundred times bigger than
      one emoji, but a photo is tens of thousands of times bigger than a text
      message, because a photo has to describe millions of individual pixels
      instead of a few dozen characters. Then the ladder flattens out again:
      a song and a photo take up roughly the same space, because both are a
      few seconds to minutes of compressed detail. The real surprise is at
      the top — all of English Wikipedia's text, the sum of millions of
      articles written by hundreds of thousands of people, is only a handful
      of movies' worth of bytes. Video is expensive because every one of its
      frames is a photo; text stays cheap no matter how much of it there is.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import { initByteScale } from "../scripts/byte-scale";
  initByteScale();
</script>

<style>
  .ladder {
    text-align: center;
  }

  .trail {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    min-height: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .trail-icon {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  .scale-stage {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: center;
    height: 12rem;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .current-icon {
    line-height: 1;
    transition: font-size 0.2s ease;
  }

  .rung-bytes {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .rung-fact {
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 32rem;
    margin: var(--space-2) auto var(--space-4);
  }

  .ladder-controls {
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .step-status {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .btn:disabled {
    opacity: 0.4;
    cursor: not-allowed;
  }

  @media (width <= 480px) {
    .scale-stage {
      height: 9rem;
    }
  }
</style>
